# Go standards

Target Go 1.27. Prefer current stdlib and language features over older patterns.

- Use generic functions and methods when they remove duplication; do not force generics onto a single concrete type.
- Prefer `slices`, `maps`, `cmp`, `log/slog`, `encoding/json/v2`, and `uuid` over hand-rolled or third-party equivalents.
- Never import `"log"`. Use `log/slog` and `github.com/lordnynex/mcp/lib/logger`.
- Accept `context.Context` as the first parameter of blocking or cancelable functions.
- Wrap errors with `%w`. Return sentinel or typed errors callers can inspect.
- Define interfaces at the consumer, not the producer.
- Run `gofmt` and `go vet` on changed packages.

```go
// ❌ BAD
func keys(m map[string]int) []string {
    out := make([]string, 0, len(m))
    for k := range m {
        out = append(out, k)
    }
    return out
}

// ✅ GOOD
func keys[M ~map[K]V, K comparable, V any](m M) []K {
    return slices.Collect(maps.Keys(m))
}
```
